{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Here I am raining down for you bread from heaven.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): He called the manna 'bread' because they would make bread from it, for He was not raining down the bread itself — and it is like the matter written (Job 28:5): ״ארץ ממנה יצא לחם״ — whose sense is that from it comes forth the wheat from which they make bread." "<b>And know</b> that the manna was a thing gathered like tiny grains, and therefore Scripture used the language of gathering regarding it: <b>let them gather it each day's portion,</b> and it was like peeled, round seeds, from what is written: דק מחוספס, and the meaning of מחוספס is 'peeled,' from the root of מחשוף הלבן ('the exposing of the white'), for the samekh is doubled, and so Onkelos translated: דעדק מקלף, and the second דק he translated דעדק כגיר, for it was made of confections, and thus he translated חמרים — דגורין; and it was white like כפור, and it was sweet, and one verse encompasses all three and explains that it was round and white and sweet — that is what is written: ״והוא כזרע גד לבן וטעמו כצפיחת בדבש״. And perhaps the doubling of the samekh comes to hint at what R. Eliezer ha-Moda'i would expound: the height of the manna was sixty se'ah — it is derived from פתיחה פתיחה: פתיחה is written regarding the flood (Genesis 7:11) ״וארבות השמים נפתחו״, and פתיחה is written regarding the manna (Psalms 78:23) ״ודלתי שמים פתח״; and so it is found in tractate Yoma, at the beginning of the chapter Yom ha-Kippurim. And it would descend at night while they slept on their beds, so that they would rise early in the morning and find their sustenance prepared — and this is among the supervised preparations of the Master of supervisions, blessed be He. And similar to this was the case of the building of the Holy Temple, may it be rebuilt, where rain would fall on Wednesday nights and Sabbath nights and not by day, so that they would not be prevented from their labor; and likewise with Hezekiah, who said to the Holy One, blessed be He: 'I have no strength to slay and pursue, nor to recite song, but I lie sleeping on my bed and You act' — He said to him: 'I act,' as it is written (II Kings 19:35): ״ויהי בלילה ההוא ויצא מלאך ה' ויך במחנה אשור וגו'״, as they expounded in Megillat Eikhah Rabbati." "<b>And you will find</b> one verse that makes all this clear (Psalms 127:2): ״כן יתן לידידו שנא״ — for He, exalted be He, gives to His beloved in the hour of sleep that which those who rise early attain after resting, after toil and labor, since they still eat in pain; if so, it is futile for those who rise early to go through all that trouble, for what they find after the trouble — the Holy One, blessed be He, gives it to His beloved in the hour of sleep without trouble. And what is written about it (Numbers 11:7): ״ועינו כעין הבדולח״, indicates that it is a product of the supernal light, of which it is written (Ezekiel 1:22): ״כעין הקרח הנורא״. And the בדולח they explained to be the jewel called פירל״א, upon which Zebulun was engraved, and so it is written in Sefer Mikhlol under the root בדולח. And the manna would descend like a fine rain, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 147:16–17): ״כפור כאפר יפזר … משליך קרחו כפתים״; and therefore David, peace be upon him, explained (Psalms 78): ״ויצו שחקים ממעל ודלתי שמים פתח״, and it is written (ibid.): ״וימטר עליהם מן לאכול ודגן שמים נתן למו״. And this indicates that the supernal light became corporealized by the will of the Holy One, blessed be He, and therefore David, peace be upon him, called it דגן שמים, and he further noted that He rained it down by opening His doors, for thus we find regarding the supernal light (Ezekiel 1:1): ״נפתחו השמים ואראה מראות אלהים״. And you will find here, in the passage of the manna, the word מן five times, corresponding to what Scripture mentions in the passage of Genesis the word אור five times — and this is to hint that the manna is a product of light and light is the foundation of the manna; and just as light was created on the first day, so the beginning of the manna's descent was on the first day. And they expounded (Psalms 78:25): ״לחם אבירים אכל איש״ — 'bread of the mighty' — bread that the ministering angels eat; these are the words of R. Akiva. R. Yishmael said to him: 'You have erred! Do the ministering angels eat bread? But is it not written (Deuteronomy 9:9) ״לחם לא אכלתי ומים לא שתיתי״?' Rather, לחם אבירים — bread that is absorbed in the limbs (אברים). And from here the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: מחספס in gematria is 248 (רמ״ח) limbs; and from here that they had no need for their excretory organs, for their sustenance was absorbed in the 248 limbs that they ate. And so the Rabbis, of blessed memory, further expounded on the verse (Numbers 14:11): ״עד אנה ינאצוני העם הזה וגו' בכל האותות אשר עשיתי בקרבו״ — what is 'בקרבו'? In their intestines (בני מעים) — that they ate and did not excrete anything." "<b>And the Ramban,</b> of blessed memory, wrote that the intent of this midrash of R. Akiva and R. Yishmael is well known — that the existence of the ministering angels is in the radiance of the Shekhinah, as the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded (Nehemiah 9:6): ״ואתה מחיה את כלם״ — 'You give life to all of them' — You give life to them all; and of it is said (Ecclesiastes 11:7): ״ומתוק האור״ — for they attain in it goodly flavor. And according to the midrash of R. Akiva, the men of the manna and the ministering angels would be sustained from one thing, and therefore R. Yishmael took issue with him, for the existence of the ministering angels is in the supernal light itself, whereas R. Akiva placed existence in a thing that is corporealized from a product of light, called 'bread from heaven'; and according to his words there would be no difference between the sustenance of the men of the manna and the sustenance of the ministering angels — all would be one, and that is not so." "<b>And know</b> that from the moment Israel attained the radiance of the Shekhinah at the Sea, from that hour their soul was elevated to cleave to His products, which is the manna, and they were worthy to be given this sustenance in the wilderness. This is the intent of the Rav, with some of his own language." "<b>And it would seem</b> proper to say that what we find in this pericah in this language — as it is written, 'This is the thing that HaShem has commanded: gather of it' etc. — alludes to 'This is my El and I will glorify Him,' which was said at the Sea, for from that time they were worthy of this. And you need to know that just as the manna was a bodily food, enduring, an offshoot of the supernal light, and was given to the recipients of the Torah in order to purify their intellect and elevate their grasp in the knowledge of the Master, blessed be He, so too we find bodily foods that endure — they are living creatures prepared for the world to come — and the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to make from them a feast for the righteous; and perhaps they too are an offshoot of the supernal light, and therefore they endure. And these are the fish whose name is Leviathan, the bird whose name is Bar Yochani, and both were created on the fifth day. And Bar Yochani is so called because it is prepared (מוכן) for the feast of the righteous; and likewise the Behemoth on a thousand hills, created on the sixth day, which is prepared for the day He decreed for a thousand generations. And there is no doubt that these are fine and pure foods that sharpen the intellect like the manna, and more than it, for in that time there will be an abundance of wisdom, as it is said (Isaiah 11:9): 'כי מלאה הארץ דעה את ה' כמים לים מכסים.'" "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> 'Behold, I will rain down for you bread from heaven' — how beloved Israel was before Him, for He changed for them the act of Creation. In the way of the world, dew descends from the heavens and bread grows from the earth; but now bread descended from the heavens and the dew rose from the earth." "<b>And the people went out and gathered.</b> That they would go outside the camp into the wildernesses. <b>A day's portion in its day</b> — that He who created the day created its sustenance. From here Rabbi Elazar HaModa'i would say: 'Whoever has what to eat today and says, What will I eat tomorrow — behold, this one is of little faith.' And the explanation of his words is that one who says so shows of himself that his trust is only in what he sees under his hand, and therefore he is of little faith, for the essence of faith is to believe what a person does not see — to hang his trust upon the Master of mercy who created him and his day, that He will have mercy upon him and give him his sustenance." "<b>And in tractate</b> Yoma, beginning of the chapter 'Yom HaKippurim,' the disciples asked Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai: Why did the manna not descend for Israel once a year? He said to them: In order that each one of Israel would worry and say, Perhaps tomorrow the manna will not descend, and my sons and daughters will die of hunger — and so they would all direct their hearts to their Father in heaven. Another interpretation: In order that they would eat it while it was hot. Another interpretation: Because of the burden of the road. And further there: The righteous ate it as bread; the intermediate ones (Numbers 11:8) 'ועשו אותו עגות'; the wicked (ibid.) 'וטחנו ברחים או דכו במדוכה.' The righteous — it descended at the entrance of their house, as it is said (ibid.) 'וברדת הטל על המחנה לילה' etc. The intermediate ones — they would go out and gather, as it is said 'ויצא העם ולקטו.' The wicked — (ibid.) 'שטו העם ולקטו' etc. And so David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 78:27): 'וימטר עליהם כעפר שאר' etc. — these are the righteous; 'ויפל בקרב מחנהו' — these are the intermediate ones; 'סביב למשכנותיו' — these are the wicked." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah:</b> 'Behold, I will rain down for you bread from heaven' — the language of ממטיר is from the language of משמרת (watch), and it is the attribute of mercy that receives from the heavens, of which Solomon mentioned in his wisdom (Proverbs 31:15): 'ותקם בעוד לילה ותתן טרף לביתה וחק לנערותיה.' And the language of הנני is 'standing,' and the language of ממטיר goes forth to the Shekhinah, for the designated Name acts so that it rains. And Scripture did not need to mention 'the heavens,' for it is known that all rain is from the heavens; rather, the intention is that this attribute — from which sustenance comes to the entire world, both among the supernal beings and the lower ones — receives from the heavens, that is, from mercy. And this, in my opinion, is (Job 39:27): 'אם על פיך יגביה נשר,' which our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted of the Shekhinah, and immediately it mentions (ibid.): 'משם חפר אוכל למרחוק עיניו יביטו.' And so Solomon mentioned (Proverbs 31:14): 'ממרחק תביא לחמה' — that is, from there sustenance comes. And this attribute Solomon called, in the Song of Songs (5), 'אחותי רעיתי,' and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'רעיתי — פרנסתי,' for to Her they would bring the offerings, and from there the sustenance and blessing flowed upon all the world. And the manna would descend in the third watch of the night, in that watch in which the attribute of mercy keeps watch — for the night consists of three watches, corresponding to גדולה, גבורה, and תפארת. And the Sages gave a mnemonic for the first watch: חמור נוער; the second: כלבים צועקים; the third: תינוק יונק משדי אמו — that is, it is the time of sustenance for all who come into the world, for it is supplied at the time when the attribute of mercy is active. And from here you will understand that the manna's root is from the influx of mercy, which is light, and this is the meaning of מן השמים, as in (I Kings 8:32) 'ואתה תשמע השמים,' and it is written (Psalms 78:24) 'ודגן שמים נתן למו.' And it was at the time of the descent of the dew, as it is written (Numbers 11:9): 'וברדת הטל על המחנה לילה ירד המן עליו' — but Israel would gather it by day, as it is said: 'וילקטו אותו בבקר בבקר,' and it is written: 'ולחם בבקר לשבוע.'" "<b>That I may test them, whether they will walk in My Torah or not.</b> The verse teaches you that whoever has his sustenance in abundance is obligated to engage in Torah — this is what it says, 'That I may test them, whether they will walk in My Torah or not.' And this is why Torah merited the priestly gifts for the priests and the Levites, namely terumah and tithes, as it is written (II Chronicles 31:4): ״ויאמר לעם ליושבי ירושלם לתת מנת הכהנים והלוים למען יחזקו בתורת ה׳״. And corresponding to the priestly gifts, the Sages established gifts for the wise of Israel who engage in Torah, as it is said (Proverbs 31:14): ״היתה כאניות סוחר ממרחק תביא לחמה״." "<b>And know</b> that the essence of the test whereby the Holy One, blessed be He, tests His creatures is in order to make known the faith, inasmuch as He, blessed be He, rewards the righteous and punishes those who transgress His will. The test is not needed for Him, blessed be He, for He knows the secrets of the heart; rather, it is to make known to the creatures of the world, who do not know. For this reason the Holy One, blessed be He, tests the righteous, as it is said (Psalms 11:5): ״ה׳ צדיק יבחן״ — so that from this the greatness of the obligation of divine service may be made known when He stands over the trial. We also find that the Holy One, blessed be He, tests all people, the rich and the poor alike, by way of reward and punishment: He tests the rich — how he conducts himself with his wealth and the bounty bestowed upon him, whether he gives charity or not; and also the poor — whether he bears his poverty and want without weakening the power of his fear of Heaven, or whether he kicks against it. And needless to say, the soul in the body, which enters into it for a trial, in order to test the man — whether he draws his body after the counsel of his soul, or draws his soul after the lusts of his body. And in all these matters in which a man is tested, the faith of the Torah in punishment and reward is made known to creatures, and the name of Heaven is sanctified in the world, and the reward of divine service and the commandment is recognized — that He, blessed be He, either rewards a man or punishes him, in this world and in the world to come, all according to his deeds."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:00:56.258975181Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}